From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 14:26:48 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] v4l2: convert to P010 for an HEVC Main 10 encoder

The Main 10 encoder lists COLOR_FormatSurface, but FormatConverter made only
NV12 and I420, so any producer other than a 10-bit decoder failed: a client
with 8-bit YUV frames, and RGBA_1010102, which the framework maps as a 10-bit
YUVA block that the component did not accept at all.

FormatConverter now writes P010. 8-bit YUV 4:2:0, planar or semiplanar in
either chroma order, is widened by shifting every sample left by eight, which
keeps limited-range black and white at 64 and 940. Packed RGB, 8-bit and
RGBA_1010102 alike, is converted with the matrix and range of the colour
aspects the client configured, by the rule of the software AV1 and APV
encoders (BT.709 for MATRIX_BT709 and BT.2020 for any other, full range for
RANGE_FULL and limited range for any other); the chroma of each 2x2 block is
the mean of its pixels. libyuv would have converted 8-bit RGB with BT.601
limited-range weights whatever the stream says, which does not match the colour
description of the stream. A P010 block that the encoder already takes passes
through as it is.

The layout of an RGBA_1010102 block (YUVA, one root plane of 32-bit words with
10-bit samples) is named XB30 and goes to the converter, which writes P010.
The converter gets the colour aspects when the encoder creates it. The pixel
format names of RGBA and P010 had been those of BGRA.
